  "ai_summary": "Great Britain's wind generation currently stands at 5.7 GW, with Scotland providing an estimated 3.0 GW, corresponding to 29.7% of installed Scottish wind capacity. This robust renewable output delivers an exceptionally low Scottish carbon intensity of 4 gCO2/kWh, contrasting sharply with 181 gCO2/kWh across the GB grid. However, severe cross-border transmission limits prevent this clean electricity from reaching southern demand centres. \n\nThrough the Balancing Mechanism, 8,648 MWh of Scottish generation has been curtailed so far today, incurring constraint payments of about \u00a3269,689. To replace this blocked zero-carbon power, thermal generators elsewhere burned an estimated 1,643,025 m3 of gas, emitting an additional 3,027 tonnes of CO2. \n\nIf today's constraint expenditure is annualized, the yearly cost of curtailment reaches approximately \u00a398 million. Spread across Great Britain's total annual electricity consumption, this structural grid bottleneck adds an estimated \u00a30.36/MWh to consumer invoices nationwide, demonstrating how network congestion creates simultaneous financial and environmental costs for the entire energy system."
